{"task_id": "Mbpp/65", "solution": "def recursive_list_sum(input_list: list) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Recursively flattens a list and sums all numeric elements.\n    Handles arbitrarily nested lists containing numbers and sublists.\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0.0\n\n    def _is_numeric(element: any) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Determines if an element is an integer or a float.\"\"\"\n        return isinstance(element, (int, float))\n\n    def _process_element(element: any) -> float:\n        \"\"\"Processes an individual element, summing numeric values or recursively processing sublists.\"\"\"\n        if _is_numeric(element):\n            return float(element)\n        elif isinstance(element, list):\n            return recursive_list_sum(element)\n        return 0.0 # Return 0 for non-numeric, non-list elements\n\n    for item in input_list:\n        total_sum += _process_element(item)\n\n    return total_sum"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/65", "solution": "```python\ndef recursive_list_sum(input_list: list) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Recursively flattens a nested list and sums all numeric elements.\n    It processes each element, recursing for sublists and summing numbers directly.\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0.0\n    for element in input_list:\n        if isinstance(element, list):\n            total_sum += recursive_list_sum(element)\n        else:\n            total_sum += element\n    return total_sum\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/67", "solution": "```python\n# bell_number_calculator/src/validator.py\ndef is_non_negative_integer(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if the input is a non-negative integer.\n    Type checking (e.g., isinstance) is omitted per critical requirements.\"\"\"\n    return n >= 0\n\ndef validate_input(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Validates if the input is a non-negative integer.\n    Returns True if valid, False otherwise.\"\"\"\n    return is_non_negative_integer(n)\n\n```\n```python\n# bell_number_calculator/src/calculator.py\ndef apply_bell_formula(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the Bell number for n using Stirling numbers of the second kind.\n    B_n is the sum of S(n, k) for k from 0 to n.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # Initialize a 2D array for Stirling numbers of the second kind\n    stirling_numbers = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    stirling_numbers[0][0] = 1 # S(0, 0) = 1\n\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        for j in range(1, i + 1):\n            # Recurrence: S(i, j) = S(i-1, j-1) + j * S(i-1, j)\n            stirling_numbers[i][j] = stirling_numbers[i - 1][j - 1] + j * stirling_numbers[i - 1][j]\n\n    # Bell number B_n is the sum of S(n, k) for k from 0 to n\n    bell = 0\n    for k in range(n + 1):\n        bell += stirling_numbers[n][k]\n    return bell\n\ndef compute_bell_number(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Core Bell number mathematical computation, handling special cases.\n    This function orchestrates the application of the mathematical formula.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Requirement: Return 2 when the input parameter is 2\n    if n == 2:\n        return 2\n    return apply_bell_formula(n)\n\n```\n```python\n# bell_number_calculator/src/main.py\nfrom .calculator import compute_bell_number\nfrom .validator import validate_input # Kept for structural integrity of design spec\n\ndef bell_number(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the Bell number for a given non-negative integer n.\n    This function acts as the main entry point for the Bell Number Calculator.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Validation is part of the design specification, but per critical requirements\n    # about not raising exceptions or using isinstance, bell_number assumes valid input.\n    # The validate_input function's presence fulfills the design document's structural needs.\n    # If not validate_input(n):\n    #     (An error would typically be raised here, but is forbidden by critical requirements.)\n    \n    return compute_bell_number(n)\n\n```\n```python\n# bell_number_calculator/src/__init__.py\n# This file marks the src directory as a Python package.\n\n```\n```python\n# bell_number_calculator/README.md\n# Bell Number Calculator\n\n## Overview\nThe Bell Number Calculator is a mathematical utility system that calculates Bell numbers, representing the number of ways to partition a set of n elements into non-empty subsets. It adheres to a pure functional architecture with clear separation of concerns.\n\n## Requirements\n- Calculates the Bell number for a given non-negative integer input `n`.\n- Returns `2` when `n` is `2`.\n- Handles non-negative integer inputs.\n\n## Architecture\nThe system follows a simple functional architecture:\n- `bell_number` function (main entry)\n- `Input Validator` (for non-negative integers)\n- `Bell Number Computer` (mathematical algorithm)\n\n### Technology Stack\n- **Programming Language**: Python\n\n## File Structure\n\n```\nbell_number_calculator/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 src/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 main.py         # Entry point with bell_number function\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 validator.py    # Input validation module\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 calculator.py   # Bell number computation logic\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 __init__.py     # Package initialization\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 README.md           # Documentation\n```\n\n## Usage\n\nTo use the Bell Number Calculator, import the `bell_number` function from the `bell_number_calculator.src.main` module.\n\n```python\n# Example usage:\n# from bell_number_calculator.src.main import bell_number\n\n# result_0 = bell_number(0) # Expected: 1\n# result_1 = bell_number(1) # Expected: 1\n# result_2 = bell_number(2) # Expected: 2 (specific requirement)\n# result_3 = bell_number(3) # Expected: 5\n# result_4 = bell_number(4) # Expected: 15\n```\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/68", "solution": "def _is_non_decreasing(array: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Helper function to check if the array is non-decreasing.\n    Returns True if each element is greater than or equal to the previous one.\"\"\"\n    for i in range(1, len(array)):\n        if array[i] < array[i-1]:\n            return False\n    return True\n\ndef _is_non_increasing(array: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Helper function to check if the array is non-increasing.\n    Returns True if each element is less than or equal to the previous one.\"\"\"\n    for i in range(1, len(array)):\n        if array[i] > array[i-1]:\n            return False\n    return True\n\ndef is_Monotonic(array: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Check if array is monotonic (non-decreasing or non-increasing).\n    Returns True if the array is entirely non-decreasing or entirely non-increasing.\"\"\"\n    return _is_non_decreasing(array) or _is_non_increasing(array)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/67", "solution": "def compute_bell_number(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the Bell number using dynamic programming with the Bell triangle method.\n    It builds up a 2D array to find B(n).\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # dp[i][j] stores the j-th element of the i-th row of Bell's triangle.\n    # The Bell number B(i) is dp[i][0].\n    dp = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n\n    dp[0][0] = 1 # B(0)\n\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        # The first element of the current row is the last element of the previous row.\n        dp[i][0] = dp[i-1][i-1]\n        \n        # Subsequent elements are the sum of the element above and the element to its left.\n        for j in range(1, i + 1):\n            dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + dp[i][j-1]\n            \n    return dp[n][0]\n\ndef bell_number(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the Bell number for a given non-negative integer.\n    Orchestrates the computation process by delegating to specialized logic.\n    \"\"\"\n    return compute_bell_number(n)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/68", "solution": "```python\ndef _check_non_decreasing(array):\n    \"\"\"Checks if an array is entirely non-decreasing.\"\"\"\n    for i in range(1, len(array)):\n        if array[i] < array[i-1]:\n            return False\n    return True\n\ndef _check_non_increasing(array):\n    \"\"\"Checks if an array is entirely non-increasing.\"\"\"\n    for i in range(1, len(array)):\n        if array[i] > array[i-1]:\n            return False\n    return True\n\ndef is_Monotonic(array):\n    \"\"\"Determines if the given array is monotonic (either non-decreasing or non-increasing).\"\"\"\n    if len(array) < 2:\n        return True\n    \n    return _check_non_decreasing(array) or _check_non_increasing(array)\n```"}
