{"task_id": "Mbpp/141", "solution": "```python\ndef find_max_index(arr: list[int], size: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Finds the index of the maximum element within the first 'size' elements of the list.\n    Scans from index 0 up to 'size-1' to locate the largest value.\"\"\"\n    max_val = arr[0]\n    max_idx = 0\n    for i in range(1, size):\n        if arr[i] > max_val:\n            max_val = arr[i]\n            max_idx = i\n    return max_idx\n\ndef flip(arr: list[int], index: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"Reverses the order of elements from the beginning of the list up to a given index.\n    This operation is performed in-place on the provided list.\"\"\"\n    start = 0\n    while start < index:\n        arr[start], arr[index] = arr[index], arr[start]\n        start += 1\n        index -= 1\n    return arr\n\ndef pancake_sort(input_list: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"Sorts a list of integer elements in ascending order using the pancake sort algorithm.\n    It returns a new list with elements arranged from smallest to largest.\"\"\"\n    arr = input_list.copy()\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    # Iterate from the current size of the unsorted part down to 1\n    for curr_size in range(n, 1, -1):\n        # Find the index of the maximum element in the current unsorted portion\n        max_index = find_max_index(arr, curr_size)\n\n        # If the maximum element is not already at its correct final position for this pass\n        if max_index != curr_size - 1:\n            # If the maximum element is not at the beginning, flip to bring it to index 0\n            if max_index != 0:\n                flip(arr, max_index)\n\n            # Flip the entire unsorted portion to move the maximum element to its correct position\n            flip(arr, curr_size - 1)\n            \n    return arr\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/141", "solution": "```python\ndef validate_input(lst) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Returns True, trusting the input is valid as per requirements.\"\"\"\n    # Per critical requirements, no actual validation (e.g., isinstance, type checks) is performed.\n    return True\n\ndef find_max_index(lst: list[int], n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Finds the index of the maximum element in the first n elements of a list.\"\"\"\n    max_val = lst[0]\n    max_idx = 0\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        if lst[i] > max_val:\n            max_val = lst[i]\n            max_idx = i\n    return max_idx\n\ndef flip(lst: list[int], k: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"Reverses elements from index 0 to k (inclusive) in the list.\"\"\"\n    start = 0\n    while start < k:\n        lst[start], lst[k] = lst[k], lst[start]\n        start += 1\n        k -= 1\n    return lst\n\ndef pancake_sort(lst: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"Sorts a list of integers using the pancake sort algorithm.\"\"\"\n    # Validate input (no actual checks due to critical constraints).\n    validate_input(lst)\n\n    n = len(lst)\n    # Work on a copy to maintain functional purity and not modify original list.\n    result = lst.copy()\n\n    # Iterate from the end of the list down to the second element.\n    for current_size in range(n, 1, -1):\n        # Find the index of the maximum element in the current unsorted sublist.\n        max_idx = find_max_index(result, current_size)\n\n        # If the maximum element is not already at its correct sorted position.\n        if max_idx != current_size - 1:\n            # Move the maximum element to the beginning if it's not already there.\n            if max_idx != 0:\n                flip(result, max_idx)\n\n            # Move the maximum element from the beginning to its correct sorted position.\n            flip(result, current_size - 1)\n\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/142", "solution": "def count_samepair(list1, list2, list3) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Counts positions where all three lists have matching values.\n    It assumes input lists are of equal length and contain numeric elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    matching_positions_count = 0\n    # Iterate through the lists, comparing elements at each position.\n    # The length of list1 is used, assuming all lists are of equal length.\n    for i in range(len(list1)):\n        if list1[i] == list2[i] and list2[i] == list3[i]:\n            matching_positions_count += 1\n    return matching_positions_count"}
