{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "def max_Product(array: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the pair of integers with the highest product from a given array.\n    Iterates through all possible pairs to identify the one with the maximum product.\n    \"\"\"\n    max_prod = float('-inf')\n    result_pair = (None, None)\n\n    num_elements = len(array)\n    for i in range(num_elements):\n        for j in range(i + 1, num_elements):\n            num1 = array[i]\n            num2 = array[j]\n            current_prod = num1 * num2\n\n            if current_prod > max_prod:\n                max_prod = current_prod\n                result_pair = (num1, num2)\n\n    return result_pair"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/418", "solution": "```python\ndef Find_Max(input_list: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds and returns the element with maximum length from a list of sublists.\n    Returns the first element if multiple elements have equal maximum length.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not input_list:\n        return []\n\n    max_element = input_list[0]\n    max_length = len(input_list[0])\n\n    for i in range(1, len(input_list)):\n        current_element = input_list[i]\n        current_length = len(current_element)\n\n        if current_length > max_length:\n            max_length = current_length\n            max_element = current_element\n\n    return max_element\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "```python\ndef max_Product(array: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    # This function finds the pair of integers with the highest product from a given array.\n    # It tracks the two largest and two smallest numbers to determine the maximum product.\n    \n    max1 = float('-inf')  # Stores the largest number found so far\n    max2 = float('-inf')  # Stores the second largest number found so far\n    min1 = float('inf')   # Stores the smallest number found so far\n    min2 = float('inf')   # Stores the second smallest number found so far\n\n    for num in array:\n        # Update the two largest numbers\n        if num >= max1:\n            max2 = max1\n            max1 = num\n        elif num > max2:\n            max2 = num\n\n        # Update the two smallest numbers\n        if num <= min1:\n            min2 = min1\n            min1 = num\n        elif num < min2:\n            min2 = num\n    \n    # Compare the product of the two largest numbers with the product of the two smallest numbers.\n    # The pair with the highest product is returned, sorted from smaller to larger.\n    if max1 * max2 >= min1 * min2:\n        return (max2, max1)\n    else:\n        return (min1, min2)\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/420", "solution": "def cube_Sum(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of cubes of the first n even natural numbers.\n    It uses a mathematical formula for efficient computation.\n    \"\"\"\n    # The sum of cubes of the first n even natural numbers is 2 * n^2 * (n + 1)^2.\n    # This derives from sum((2i)^3 for i=1 to n) = 8 * sum(i^3 for i=1 to n)\n    # And sum(i^3 for i=1 to n) = (n * (n + 1) / 2)^2.\n    \n    # n * (n + 1) will always be an even number, so integer division by 2 is exact.\n    term_n_plus_1 = n + 1\n    \n    # Calculate n^2 * (n + 1)^2\n    result = 2 * (n * n) * (term_n_plus_1 * term_n_plus_1)\n    \n    return result"}
