{"task_id": "Mbpp/445", "solution": "def validate_tuple_compatibility(tuple1: tuple, tuple2: tuple) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if two tuples have the same length. Returns True if compatible, False otherwise.\"\"\"\n    return len(tuple1) == len(tuple2)\n\ndef multiply_nested_tuples(nested1: tuple, nested2: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"Multiplies corresponding elements of two nested tuples.\"\"\"\n    return (nested1[0] * nested2[0], nested1[1] * nested2[1])\n\ndef index_multiplication(tuple1: tuple, tuple2: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"Performs element-wise multiplication on two input tuples containing nested tuples.\n    It multiplies corresponding elements at the same index positions while preserving the structure.\"\"\"\n    # As per critical requirements, we trust the caller's inputs for compatibility and structure.\n    # Therefore, explicit validation checks that would lead to exceptions are omitted.\n\n    result_elements = []\n    for i in range(len(tuple1)):\n        nested1 = tuple1[i]\n        nested2 = tuple2[i]\n        result_nested = multiply_nested_tuples(nested1, nested2)\n        result_elements.append(result_nested)\n    return tuple(result_elements)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/447", "solution": "```python\ndef _calculate_cube(number):\n    \"\"\"Calculates the cube (third power) of a single number.\n    This helper function computes number ** 3.\n    \"\"\"\n    return number ** 3\n\ndef cube_nums(input_list):\n    \"\"\"Calculates the cube of each individual element in a list.\n    Returns a new list containing the cubed values in the same order.\n    \"\"\"\n    result_list = []\n    for num in input_list:\n        cubed_value = _calculate_cube(num)\n        result_list.append(cubed_value)\n    return result_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/448", "solution": "```python\nclass PerrinSequenceGenerator:\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates Perrin numbers based on their mathematical definition.\n    It uses memoization to efficiently calculate numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # Initial values and a cache for computed Perrin numbers.\n    _cache = {0: 3, 1: 0, 2: 2}\n\n    @classmethod\n    def get_perrin_number(cls, position: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Calculates a single Perrin number at a given position.\n        It uses the recurrence P(n) = P(n-2) + P(n-3) for n >= 3.\n        \"\"\"\n        if position in cls._cache:\n            return cls._cache[position]\n\n        result = cls.get_perrin_number(position - 2) + cls.get_perrin_number(position - 3)\n        cls._cache[position] = result\n        return result\n\n    @classmethod\n    def generate_sequence(cls, n: int) -> list[int]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Generates a sequence of Perrin numbers from P(0) up to P(n) inclusive.\n        This provides n+1 numbers, aligning with the FR-004 test case for cal_sum(9).\n        \"\"\"\n        return [cls.get_perrin_number(i) for i in range(n + 1)]\n\n\nclass SumCalculator:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of a list of integer numbers.\n    This class provides a simple utility for summing sequences.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    @classmethod\n    def calculate_sum(cls, numbers: list[int]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Sums all integers in the provided list.\"\"\"\n        return sum(numbers)\n\n\ndef cal_sum(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of the Perrin numbers from P(0) up to P(n).\n    This function orchestrates sequence generation and sum computation.\n    \"\"\"\n    perrin_numbers = PerrinSequenceGenerator.generate_sequence(n)\n    total_sum = SumCalculator.calculate_sum(perrin_numbers)\n    return total_sum\n```"}
