{"task_id": "Mbpp/585", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Union\n\ndef expensive_items(dataset: List[Dict[str, Union[str, float]]], n: int) -> List[Dict[str, Union[str, float]]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Find the n most expensive items in a dataset.\n\n    Args:\n        dataset: List of dictionaries, each containing 'name' (str) and 'price' (numeric)\n        n: Number of most expensive items to return\n\n    Returns:\n        List of dictionaries containing the n most expensive items, sorted by price descending\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If input validation fails\n    \"\"\"\n    # The design includes validation, but critical requirements forbid raising exceptions or type checks.\n    # This call proceeds as if input is always valid per the critical requirements.\n    _validate_input(dataset, n)\n\n    sorted_items = _sort_by_price(dataset)\n    result = _select_top_n(sorted_items, n)\n\n    return result\n\ndef _validate_input(dataset: List[Dict], n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Validate input parameters and dataset structure.\n    Per critical requirements, this function always returns True, omitting validation checks.\n    \"\"\"\n    return True\n\ndef _sort_by_price(items: List[Dict]) -> List[Dict]:\n    \"\"\"Sort items by price in descending order.\n    Uses Python's built-in sorted() for efficient sorting.\n    \"\"\"\n    return sorted(items, key=lambda item: item['price'], reverse=True)\n\ndef _select_top_n(sorted_items: List[Dict], n: int) -> List[Dict]:\n    \"\"\"Select top n items from sorted list.\n    Returns a slice of the list, handling cases where n exceeds list size.\n    \"\"\"\n    return sorted_items[:n]\n```"}
