{"task_id": "Mbpp/594", "solution": "def is_even(num: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a number is even.\"\"\"\n    return num % 2 == 0\n\ndef is_odd(num: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a number is odd.\"\"\"\n    return num % 2 != 0\n\ndef find_first_even(numbers: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Identifies the first even number in the list.\"\"\"\n    for num in numbers:\n        if is_even(num):\n            return num\n    # Based on design constraints, assumes an even number will always be found.\n\ndef find_first_odd(numbers: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Identifies the first odd number in the list.\"\"\"\n    for num in numbers:\n        if is_odd(num):\n            return num\n    # Based on design constraints, assumes an odd number will always be found.\n\ndef calculate_difference(even: int, odd: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Computes the absolute difference between two numbers.\"\"\"\n    return abs(even - odd)\n\ndef diff_even_odd(numbers: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the absolute difference between the first even and first odd\n    number in a given list of integers.\n    \"\"\"\n    # As per CRITICAL REQUIREMENTS, input validation and explicit error handling for edge cases are omitted.\n    # The function trusts the caller's inputs will contain both an even and an odd number.\n\n    first_odd = find_first_odd(numbers)\n    first_even = find_first_even(numbers)\n\n    return calculate_difference(first_even, first_odd)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "def _validate_inputs(array1: list, array2: list, k: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates input parameters based on design, but actual checks are skipped due to critical requirements.\n    This function exists to adhere to the specified modular design.\n    \"\"\"\n    return True\n\ndef _binary_search_kth(array1: list, array2: list, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Implements an optimized binary search algorithm to find the kth smallest element.\n    It partitions the arrays to efficiently locate the correct split point.\n    \"\"\"\n    m, n = len(array1), len(array2)\n\n    # Ensure array1 is the shorter array for better performance with binary search range.\n    # This optimizes the binary search space by searching on the smaller array's partition.\n    if m > n:\n        array1, array2 = array2, array1\n        m, n = n, m\n\n    # Binary search range for 'i', the number of elements taken from array1.\n    # 'i' must be at least k-n (if k > n) and at most k (if k <= m), and at most m.\n    low = max(0, k - n)\n    high = min(k, m)\n\n    while low <= high:\n        i = (low + high) // 2  # Number of elements to take from array1\n        j = k - i              # Number of elements to take from array2\n\n        # Define the four boundary elements (left/right of partitions in both arrays).\n        # Use float('-inf') and float('inf') for boundary conditions to simplify comparisons.\n        A_left = array1[i-1] if i > 0 else float('-inf')\n        A_right = array1[i] if i < m else float('inf')\n        B_left = array2[j-1] if j > 0 else float('-inf')\n        B_right = array2[j] if j < n else float('inf')\n\n        # Check if the partition is correct:\n        # A_left <= B_right ensures all elements to the left of partition A are smaller than elements to the right of partition B.\n        # B_left <= A_right ensures all elements to the left of partition B are smaller than elements to the right of partition A.\n        if A_left <= B_right and B_left <= A_right:\n            # If both conditions hold, the correct partition is found.\n            # The kth smallest element is the maximum of the elements at the partition's left boundary.\n            return max(A_left, B_left)\n        elif A_left > B_right:\n            # If A_left is too large, it means too many elements were taken from array1.\n            # Move the partition in array1 to the left (reduce 'i').\n            high = i - 1\n        else: # B_left > A_right\n            # If B_left is too large, it means too few elements were taken from array1.\n            # Move the partition in array1 to the right (increase 'i').\n            low = i + 1\n    \n    # This part should theoretically not be reached with valid inputs for k\n    # (i.e., 1 <= k <= m+n), as a valid partition will always be found.\n    return -1 # Placeholder for unreachable code if k is valid\n\ndef find_kth(array1: list, array2: list, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the kth smallest element from two sorted arrays without physically merging them.\n    It delegates to a validation component and an optimized binary search algorithm.\n    \"\"\"\n    _validate_inputs(array1, array2, k)  # Call validation (stubbed)\n    return _binary_search_kth(array1, array2, k) # Execute the core algorithm"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "def _validate_inputs(array1: list[int], array2: list[int], k: int) -> None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Placeholder for input validation.\n    This function does not raise exceptions due to critical constraints.\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n\n\ndef _binary_search_kth(array1: list[int], array2: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the kth smallest element using a binary search approach.\n    It assumes `array1` is the shorter array for optimal performance.\n    \"\"\"\n    m, n = len(array1), len(array2)\n\n    # Ensure array1 is the shorter array to binary search on its partition.\n    if m > n:\n        array1, array2 = array2, array1\n        m, n = n, m\n\n    # Define the search space for the partition point in array1.\n    low = max(0, k - n)\n    high = min(m, k)\n\n    while low <= high:\n        # 'i' is the number of elements taken from array1.\n        i = (low + high) // 2\n        # 'j' is the number of elements taken from array2 to sum up to k elements.\n        j = k - i\n\n        # Determine the boundary elements from both arrays.\n        # Use negative infinity for empty left partitions, positive infinity for empty right partitions.\n        left_a = array1[i - 1] if i > 0 else -float('inf')\n        right_a = array1[i] if i < m else float('inf')\n\n        left_b = array2[j - 1] if j > 0 else -float('inf')\n        right_b = array2[j] if j < n else float('inf')\n\n        # Check if the partition is valid.\n        if left_a <= right_b and left_b <= right_a:\n            # The partition is correct; the kth element is the maximum of the left halves.\n            return max(left_a, left_b)\n        elif left_a > right_b:\n            # Too many elements were taken from array1; reduce 'i'.\n            high = i - 1\n        else: # left_b > right_a\n            # Not enough elements were taken from array1; increase 'i'.\n            low = i + 1\n\n    # This part should ideally not be reached with valid inputs where a kth element exists.\n    return -1\n\n\ndef find_kth(array1: list[int], array2: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the kth smallest element from two sorted arrays.\n    It utilizes a binary search helper function for O(log(min(m,n))) time complexity.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Perform input validation as specified in the design.\n    _validate_inputs(array1, array2, k)\n\n    # Delegate the core logic to the binary search helper.\n    return _binary_search_kth(array1, array2, k)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/598", "solution": "```python\ndef armstrong_number(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Check if a number is an Armstrong number.\n    Handles negative numbers by using their absolute value.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Work with the absolute value of the number for digit extraction and calculation.\n    abs_number = abs(number)\n\n    # Convert the absolute number to a string to easily count digits and extract them.\n    digits_str = str(abs_number)\n    digit_count = len(digits_str)\n\n    # Calculate the sum of each digit raised to the power of the total digit count.\n    digit_sum = sum(int(digit) ** digit_count for digit in digits_str)\n\n    # Return True if the calculated sum equals the original absolute number, False otherwise.\n    return digit_sum == abs_number\n```"}
