{"task_id": "Mbpp/803", "solution": "```python\ndef _validate_input(number) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Validate that input is a valid integer for perfect square checking.\n    Perfect squares are non-negative.\n    \"\"\"\n    return number >= 0\n\ndef _calculate_integer_sqrt(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculate the integer square root of a non-negative number using binary search.\n    Assumes number is non-negative, as handled by _validate_input.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number < 2:\n        return number # Handles 0 and 1\n\n    low, high = 1, number // 2 + 1 # Optimization: root cannot exceed half the number for number > 1\n    ans = 0\n    while low <= high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if mid * mid == number:\n            return mid\n        elif mid * mid < number:\n            ans = mid\n            low = mid + 1\n        else:\n            high = mid - 1\n    return ans\n\ndef _is_perfect_square_root(number: int, sqrt_val: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Check if the square of sqrt_val exactly equals number.\n    This determines if number is a perfect square.\n    \"\"\"\n    return sqrt_val * sqrt_val == number\n\ndef is_perfect_square(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Check if the given number is a perfect square.\n\n    Args:\n        number (int): The number to check\n\n    Returns:\n        bool: True if number is perfect square, False otherwise\n    \"\"\"\n    if not _validate_input(number):\n        return False\n\n    integer_sqrt = _calculate_integer_sqrt(number)\n    return _is_perfect_square_root(number, integer_sqrt)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/804", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef is_product_even(numbers: List[int]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the product of numbers in a list is even.\n    Returns True if the product is even, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        # According to the design's empty list handling sequence diagram, return False.\n        # This aligns with the convention that an empty product is 1, which is odd.\n        return False\n\n    # The product is even if at least one number in the list is even.\n    # This includes zero, as 0 is an even number.\n    for num in numbers:\n        if num % 2 == 0:\n            return True\n\n    # If no even number was found, all numbers are odd, and their product is odd.\n    return False\n```"}
